From b8cf4f62a87ab70fadbd78227f9a65a15e2dcb74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <226450061+logdog2325@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:33:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 145/146] XD Netplay: pressing Start while the other player's
 previous battle is still closing no longer ends or desyncs the new one

NetPlayClient::StartGame enables netplay input (NetPlay_Enable) and asks the UI
to boot. On Qt, MainWindow::StartGame defers that boot while the previous
game's core is still alive; on Android the old core can also still be closing.
For that moment netplay is on and the only core running is the old one. Two
things went wrong there.

The old core's final state change ended the new game. Both platforms' state
hooks (NetPlayDialog's EmulationStateChanged handler, NetPlayUICallbacks'
state callback) call RequestStopGame on Stopping or Uninitialized unless a
stop was already requested, and BootGame had just cleared that flag for the
new game. So the old core's own Uninitialized asked the server to stop the
new game for every player. A core announces Starting first and Uninitialized
last, even when its boot fails, and the next core cannot start until the old
one has finished announcing (Core::Init joins its thread; on Qt the events
arrive in posting order). Both hooks now only stop on a Stopping or
Uninitialized that follows this game's Starting (m_game_core_seen, cleared in
BootGame), so a failed boot still ends the game as before.

The old core could read the new game's input. Its CPU thread keeps polling
SI while it closes, reaches NetPlay_GetInput and pops the next game's opening
fill: the same loss of a whole fill that desynced the GBA cores on
2026-09-22. Its frame hook also reached SendTimeBase, advancing the new game's
frame count and sending time bases the server compares. Core now counts the
cores it creates (Core::GetBootSequence, moved in Core::Init just before the
emu thread starts, never while a core is alive). OnStartGame parks the game's
number where no core can match it, and StartGame records the number the
game's core will have, current + 1, before enabling netplay input; StartGame
always runs before that core's Init on both platforms. NetPlay_GetInput,
SendTimeBase and NetPlay_GetWiimoteData turn away any other core, which then
sees a neutral pad while it finishes closing. The GBA auto-reset paths
(SI_DeviceGBAEmu) likewise stop an old core from queueing a reset on the
shared GBA pad through the new free function NetPlay::IsCurrentGameCore; it
takes crit_netplay_client, so it is only for callers outside
NetPlay_GetInput, which these are (they already call GetPadDetails there).

On desktop the new game could also simply never start. MainWindow::StartGame
defers a boot while a core is alive by calling RequestStop, which with the
default Confirm on Stop asks "A shutdown is already in progress... Force
stop?". If the old core finished while that question was open, OnStopComplete
ran before m_pending_boot was set, and the boot was stranded whatever the
answer. A netplay boot that arrives while a stop is already in progress is now
parked in m_pending_boot without asking; the Uninitialized that ends the old
game reaches OnStopComplete, which starts it. Solo play still asks as before.

SendTimeBase also gains the null check it always needed: its caller tests
IsNetPlayRunning() before taking crit_netplay_client.

The padpop line reports stale=, the polls turned away from an old core in
this game, so the field logs show whether the race was hit.
